"""Build, download and check the downloadable applications of one board.

It flashes the board's system image as board-matrix does (one configuration:
a compiler, a C library and a mode), then takes every application that
Applications/uKOS_Appls_Downloadable/apps.yaml lists for the board, builds it
against that image, resets the board, streams the .hex through `hexloader` and
checks what the application prints against its spec,
apps/<group>/<app>.json.

Every application gets a reset first: hexloader keeps the user memory reserved
once it has started an application, because the processes it created may still
run from there, so a second download would be refused until a reset. An
application is also linked against the variant's Artefacts/FLASH.elf and
carries its signature, so it is built after the flash, against the image that
is on the board; a mismatch shows as "failed to find the application
signature!".

A spec holds one or more rows, each one download:

  id        "<group>/<app>/<what>"
  args      arguments after `hexloader`, handed on to the application
  expect    patterns that must all appear in the console output, within
            timeout; {"re": pattern, "min": n} must match at least n times
            (never write that as a (?s)(...){n} pattern: it backtracks
            exponentially over a long output)
  refute    patterns that must not appear, from the download to the end
  timeout   seconds for the expect patterns (default 15)
  settle    seconds to keep watching once they all matched (default 2)
  input     steps after the download: {"write": text}, {"await": re, "timeout":
            s}, {"sleep": s}, or {"jlink": [commands]}, run through a J-Link
            core other than the application's (a J-Link board only); a
            write with "on": "dump" goes to the session of the dump console,
            the wire of urt0 where the console runs elsewhere (Asmodee_H747)
  dump      patterns of a core dump or panic the application causes on
            purpose, matched against the console and the dump console (a
            panic goes out through cmns_send(), on the Firefly_H757 the CM4's
            wire); any other core dump is a crash
  console   false when the application keeps or kills the console, so nothing
            is asked of it afterwards (default true: `dumplog` must show no
            record above Info but those matching a "log_allow" pattern, and
            every "log_expect" pattern)
  after     console commands once the window is over, each {"send": cmd,
            "expect": [re], "refute": [re]} (`process` listing the
            application's processes is how a silent one shows it runs)
  requires  "hw:<tag>" (needs --have <tag>) and "unsafe" (needs
            --allow-unsafe), as in the CLI specs
  boards    the boards the row applies to (default: all that build the app)
  except    boards the row does not apply to

Patterns are multiline Python regexes over the output with line endings
normalised to \\n, as in the CLI specs. Known failures are listed in
APP-KNOWN-FAILURES, in the format of KNOWN-FAILURES; a listed row that passes
fails the run.

--record DIR runs the applications without a spec too (or all of them with
--record-all) and keeps their output for --record-time seconds in DIR, which is
how a spec is written: from what the application really prints.

Exit codes: 0 every row passed or is known, 1 setup error or refusal,
3 at least one row failed.
"""

